module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Compile

let content(): String =
  """# Compiling to an executable

`dark compile <fn>` writes a native executable that runs as `dark run <fn>` does.
It is experimental.

  $ dark compile Me.Tools.wc
  ./wc
  341 KB, 2.1 s
  $ ./wc notes.txt
  112 notes.txt

## Which fns

The fns `dark run` runs: one that takes List<String>, the command-line arguments,
or nothing, and returns Int64, the exit code, or Unit, which is 0. Others are
refused with their signature.

`dark compile --run <expression>` compiles an expression, runs it, and prints
what `dark eval` prints.

## What an executable can do

An executable has no store, traces or DB. A fn that reads the package store,
records traces or uses dark's own runtime state is refused before anything
compiles, with the calls that lead there. `dark run` runs it here.

Every access is off by default, and compiled code can't limit access yet, so an
executable gets stdin, stdout, the clock and randomness, and nothing else. A fn
that reads files, the environment or the network, or starts programs, is refused
for now. `dark effects <fn>` lists what a fn may do.

## Checking first

  $ dark compile --check Stdlib.English.toOrdinal Me.Tools.listFns
  compiles      Stdlib.English.toOrdinal   no effects
  needs dark    Me.Tools.listFns           reads the package store, via ...

`--check` exits 1 if any fn won't compile. `--json` gives one object per fn.

## Platforms

Linux x86-64 and arm64, and macOS arm64. Elsewhere, `dark compile` says so.

## Where things live

The compiler is ~/.darklang/compiler/<commit>/darkc. Its stdlib is prepared once,
in cache/: about a minute and 150 MB. The first `dark` at a terminal starts that in
the background when 6 GB of memory is free; otherwise the first compile does it.
`dark config set compile.prepare off` turns the background start off.
"""
